<reference_puzzle index="1" source="eval" puzzle_id="195c6913" lines="37-52">
<description>
Simulate particles along a path with alternating turn direction at collisions.

Input structure:
- Top-left: 2x2 monochrome blocks define a repeating color cycle (left-to-right).
- Isolated single pixels of a cycle color: start markers adjacent to the path.
- A 2x2 monochrome block of a distinct color: end marker.

Rule:
- Each start marker launches a particle moving straight, painting cycle colors in order.
- On hitting a boundary, paint that cell with the end color and turn 90°.
- Turn direction alternates: odd collisions → left (CCW), even → right (CW).
- If the scheduled turn is blocked, the particle stops.

Key insight: The alternating left/right turn rule is the core mechanism; without it the path would be trivial.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="9344f635" lines="15-30">
<description>
Extend each colored domino marker into a full row or column bar, with horizontal bars covering vertical bars at crossings.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- Every foreground object is a 4-connected same-color domino of exactly two pixels.
- A horizontal domino marks a row to paint; a vertical domino marks a column to paint.

Rule:
- Paint each marked column entirely with the color of its vertical domino marker.
- Paint each marked row entirely with the color of its horizontal domino marker.
- Rows are painted after columns, so row bars overwrite column bars at intersections.

Key insight:
- The two-pixel orientation is the instruction: horizontal markers emit row bars, vertical markers emit column bars, and the only layering cue is row-over-column.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="79cce52d" lines="29-44">
<description>
Crop off the control row and column, then cyclically roll the payload by the two marker offsets.

Input structure
- The grid is square, with a control row on top and a control column on the left.
- The control row and column share one filler color and one single-pixel marker color.
- The lower-right square is the payload and contains none of the control colors.

Rule
- Remove the top control row and left control column.
- Roll the remaining payload down by the marker's zero-based position in the left control column.
- Roll the payload right by the marker's zero-based position in the top control row.

Key insight
- The two border markers are cyclic shift amounts for the payload, not colors to preserve.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="eval" puzzle_id="64efde09" lines="68-83">
<description>
Copy colored control-ray ports from one rectangular sprite orientation to every matching transformed sprite.

Input structure:
- bg is the most common color.
- Several dense 2-by-N or N-by-2 rectangular sprites are dihedral transforms of one template.
- Isolated single-pixel control markers sit on rays projected outward from the dominant long edge of some sprites.

Rule:
- For each sprite, the active edge is the long side whose cells have the strongest same-color majority.
- A control marker seen first along an outward ray from an active-edge cell assigns its color to that template cell.
- On every transformed sprite, draw that assigned color outward from the corresponding active-edge cell through bg cells until blocked or out of bounds.

Key insight:
- The marker positions are not absolute; they label cells of a shared rectangular template, then the labels follow each sprite's rotation/reflection.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="95755ff2" lines="45-60">
<description>
Paint straight rays from colored side markers into the hollow diamond frame until an interior foreground pixel blocks each ray.

Input structure
- The background contains one complete one-pixel-thick diamond frame.
- Colored marker pixels outside the diamond sit on the top, bottom, left, or right side of the frame's bounding box.
- Any non-background, non-frame pixels inside the diamond are blockers that remain unchanged.

Rule
- Each outside marker emits inward perpendicular to its side of the diamond bounding box.
- Along that row or column, paint only background pixels strictly inside the diamond with the marker color.
- Stop a ray when it reaches an existing interior foreground pixel; keep the frame and all original colored pixels unchanged.

Key insight
- The diamond frame defines a strict interior, while the side markers are column or row emitters whose color fills the visible interior interval until blocked.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="eval" puzzle_id="c4d067a0" lines="9-23">
<description>
Complete a grid of colored blocks to match a minimap legend in the top-left corner.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Top-left corner: a small sprite of single colored pixels forming a minimap pattern on a regular lattice.
- Main area: uniformly colored square blocks (NxN) arranged on a regular grid; some positions are empty (bg).

Rule:
- The legend pixels define a color pattern; each pixel's lattice position maps to a block position in the main grid via a fixed scale factor and offset.
- Existing colored blocks confirm the scale/offset alignment.
- For every legend pixel whose corresponding grid position is empty, place a new block of that color.

Key insight: The top-left pixel pattern is a scaled-down minimap of the block layout; the mapping is inferred from blocks already present.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="1b8318e3" lines="86-105">
<description>
Move each isolated colored pixel into the one-cell frame around a grey 2x2 block.

Input structure:
- Background is the dominant color.
- Grey foreground objects are solid 2x2 anchor blocks.
- Every other non-background, non-grey cell is a colored singleton source.

Rule:
- Keep the grey anchors fixed and clear all colored singleton source pixels.
- For each singleton and each anchor, the normal destination is the cell reached by
  clamping the pixel into the anchor's one-cell outer frame.
- If a diagonally outside singleton has an exact 8-direction sightline into an anchor,
  also allow the cell just before the first grey pixel on that ray.
- Choose distinct destinations that minimize total king-move distance; at equal
  distance prefer exact sightlines, then the shorter Manhattan movement.

Key insight: The grey 2x2 blocks define magnetic frames; conflicts are resolved
structurally by nearest-frame assignment rather than by color identity.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="eval" puzzle_id="e8686506" lines="172-185">
<description>
Assemble scattered colored fragments into a left-right symmetric rectangle, jigsaw-style.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color; remaining colors form small sprite fragments.
- Optional roles: 4 singleton pixels of one color mark corners; horizontal bars mark top/bottom edges; paired vertical lines mark left/right edges.

Rule:
- Collect all non-bg connected components as jigsaw pieces.
- Determine output rectangle dimensions from total piece area.
- Pack pieces into the rectangle so it is exactly mirror-symmetric (left-right) and same-color pieces never 4-touch.

Key insight: Left-right symmetry plus the no-same-color-touching constraint make the assembly unique; corner/edge markers anchor it when present.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="eval" puzzle_id="53fb4810" lines="105-119">
<description>
Extend marker tiles outward from '+' shapes by repeating them to the grid edge.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- A dominant non-bg color forms several '+'-shaped objects.
- Small colored patches (1–few cells) sit adjacent to each arm of a '+'.

Rule:
- For each marker tile touching a '+', determine which side of the '+' it sits on.
- Project the tile away from the '+' in that direction, repeating the tile pattern periodically until the grid boundary.
- '+' pixels are never overwritten.

Key insight: Each marker's attachment side determines its extension direction; the pattern repeats periodically, not as a single copy.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="eval" puzzle_id="a395ee82" lines="9-24">
<description>
Stamp translated copies of a monochrome template guided by a singleton pixel layout.

Input structure:
- bg = background. A large monochrome shape (template, color T).
- Singleton pixels elsewhere: exactly one is color T (layout origin); the rest share color O (overlap color).
- Singletons sit on a uniform-spacing lattice.

Rule:
- Convert each singleton's offset from the origin into coarse lattice units.
- Place a template copy at each offset, scaled by template height/width.
- The origin's copy is painted O instead of T.
- Any pixel covered by two or more copies is also recolored to O.

Key insight: The single T-colored singleton identifies both the origin position and the "self" copy, which is uniquely recolored.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="eval" puzzle_id="7ed72f31" lines="8-22">
<description>
Mirror colored sprites using red (2) markers as reflection axes.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Red (2) = marker color.
- Colored sprites appear near red markers.

Rule:
- Red line within a sprite (vertical or horizontal) → mirror payload across that axis.
- Single red pixel in a mixed sprite → point-reflect payload around it.
- Isolated red pixel (alone, no payload) → point-reflect each adjacent 8-connected color component.
- Originals remain; mirrored pixels overlay.

Key insight: Red marker geometry (line vs single pixel) determines reflection type; isolated vs embedded red determines what gets reflected.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="train" puzzle_id="e4941b18" lines="8-22">
<description>
Move red into teal's top marker position, then move teal to the bottom outside corner of the grey rectangle.

Input structure
- The dominant background surrounds one filled rectangular non-background block.
- There is exactly one red marker and one teal marker, both in the row immediately above the rectangle.
- Both markers are horizontally aligned over columns inside the rectangle.

Rule
- Clear the original red marker.
- Recolor the original teal marker cell red.
- Place the teal marker beside the rectangle's bottom row, on the side indicated by the horizontal direction from red to teal.

Key insight
- The red-to-teal horizontal order chooses the rectangle side where teal exits at the bottom.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="eval" puzzle_id="a251c730" lines="8-21">
<description>
Stamp source sprites into a destination frame via color-matched singleton markers.

Input structure:
- Two bordered rectangles: source (multi-cell sprites) and destination (singleton markers).
- Each sprite has an anchor pixel — its least-frequent color. Markers share these colors.

Rule:
- Match each destination marker to the source sprite with the same anchor color.
- Translate the sprite so its anchor overlaps the marker position.
- Output is the destination rectangle, interior cleared before stamping.

Key insight: The rarest color per sprite doubles as a template key and positioning anchor.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="14" source="train" puzzle_id="68bc2e87" lines="46-60">
